# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env.
#   cp .env.example .env

# --- Configured model: Scan and Dark Desk ---
#
# NOTHING TO SET if the desk should use your local Claude Code login for these
# features. Story Draft/Redraft has its own per-run picker described below.
# Install it once and sign in:
#   npm i -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code
#   claude            (then /login)
# Your Max/Pro subscription powers the desk. No API key anywhere.
#
# Which Claude for configured-provider calls. Default claude-opus-5.
# The explicit Story "Claude Opus" choice remains Opus.
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5
#
# If the CLI is somewhere unusual, point at the binary:
# CLAUDE_CLI_PATH=C:\Users\you\AppData\Roaming\npm\node_modules\@anthropic-ai\claude-code\bin\claude.exe
# CODEX_CLI_PATH=C:\Users\you\AppData\Roaming\npm\node_modules\@openai\codex\node_modules\@openai\codex-win32-x64\vendor\x86_64-pc-windows-msvc\bin\codex.exe
# CODEX_HOME=C:\Users\you\.codex  # normally discovered; this is Codex's login/state directory
#
# Every Queue row and the workbench have a per-run Draft with AI picker.
# Automatic uses configured LLM_* exclusively when set. Otherwise it tries
# Claude Opus, then Codex Terra, chooses the first ready provider before
# enqueue, and uses it for the whole durable job. Explicit choices never fall
# back.
#
# Zen MiMo and Local Qwen were removed from the picker (2026-09-02). 0.6.10
# brought a local model back as a named pick, "Local model" -- but generic
# this time: whatever LLM_BASE_URL below already points at (llama.cpp, LM
# Studio, or any other OpenAI-compatible server), not one named model. It
# shows up in every picker (Story, Scan, Opinion, Dark Desk) once LLM_BASE_URL
# is set, with its own longer, editable timeouts. See docs/local-models.md.
#
# Take the local model out of the pickers without unsetting LLM_BASE_URL
# (the configured-gateway path for Scan/Dark Desk/Automatic keeps working):
# TOWNREPORTER_LOCAL=0
#
# These compatibility overrides change an endpoint/model identity; do not put
# secrets in the model name or URL.
# TOWNREPORTER_CODEX_TERRA_MODEL=gpt-5.6-terra
# TOWNREPORTER_CODEX_SOL_MODEL=gpt-5.6-sol
#
# Opinion is Claude only (Codex declines editorials). The picker offers
# Automatic and Claude Opus, and both mean Claude Opus, through the signed-in
# Claude Code session; Claude Code receives the voice by file path.
# Opinion needs an existing voice file at an absolute path outside this repo:
# TOWNREPORTER_VOICE_FILE=C:/Users/you/.townreporter/voice/your-voice.md
# Codex retains the signed-in user's native configuration and full available
# machine access, including search, shell/files, apps, plugins, and skills.
#
# Take the Claude Code CLI out of the runtime provider chain entirely:
# TOWNREPORTER_CLAUDE_CODE=0

# Take the Codex CLI out of the chain entirely (it stays installed).
# TOWNREPORTER_CODEX=0
#
# The ordinary `npm test` suite is always offline and starts with DATABASE_URL
# removed. Live provider evaluation is separate and explicit:
# RUN_LIVE_MODEL_TESTS=1 npm run test:live-model

# Set to 1 ONLY in the install that owns the Cloudflare tunnel. On a machine
# running more than one install (live paper + development copy), the Server
# page's tunnel status and its Restart button belong to exactly one of them;
# every other install reports "not managed by this install" and refuses the
# restart.
# TOWNREPORTER_TUNNEL=1

# --- Model: Claude by API key (optional, beats the CLI) ---
# Only if you'd rather bill an API key than use the subscription.
# Key: https://console.anthropic.com
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#
# API-key path only. How hard it thinks: low | medium | high | xhigh | max.
# Default high. Lower is cheaper and faster; higher reads better.
# ANTHROPIC_EFFORT=high

# --- Model: Grok (fallback) ---
# Only used when ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is empty. Key: https://console.x.ai
# XAI_API_KEY=
# XAI_MODEL=grok-4.5
# XAI_BASE_URL=https://api.x.ai/v1

# --- Other models: any OpenAI-compatible /v1/chat/completions gateway ---
# If LLM_BASE_URL is set (or LLM_API_KEY + LLM_MODEL are both set), this wins
# over BOTH Claude and Grok for Scan/Dark Desk and becomes the exclusive
# provider for Story Automatic.
# This is the hook for running a local model on the box instead of paying per word.
# Same three vars for LiteLLM, Bifrost, Helicone, MLflow, Kong, Ollama, OpenAI.
#
# LiteLLM:          LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1
# Bifrost:          LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1
#                   (Bifrost's own default port is 8080 — that collides with
#                   TownReporter. Map it: docker run -p 4000:8080 maximhq/bifrost)
# Helicone:         LLM_BASE_URL=https://oai.helicone.ai/v1
#                   (or your self-hosted Helicone worker)
# MLflow AI Gateway: LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5000/v1
# Kong AI Gateway:  LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1
# OpenAI:           LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# OpenRouter:       LLM_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# Ollama:           LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
#                   LLM_API_KEY=ollama
#                   LLM_MODEL=llama3.1
#
# LLM_BASE_URL=
# LLM_API_KEY=
# LLM_MODEL=

# --- Database ---
# Unset = local PGLite (data dies when the process stops).
# 5433 in SELF-HOSTING.md is that one production box's port-conflict
# workaround; a fresh machine uses the standard 5432 below.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@host:5432/townreporter

# --- Desk sign-in ---
# Self-host: open /login and create an editor account (email + password).
# That lives in YOUR database. Grok Google/X buttons only show on *.grok.me
# unless you set VITE_GROK_OAUTH=true and the GROK_AUTH_* broker vars.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:8080
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=generate-a-long-random-string
#
# Unsigned-in local editor. Do not use on a public host.
# VITE_AUTH_ENABLED=false
#
# Removed in 0.5.1. The first account to sign up owns the desk, on every host.
# A single-editor newsroom could not re-issue a lost token, so the token was a
# lock with no locksmith. Sign-in is rate limited instead: ten attempts per
# five minutes per address, always on.
#
# Background monitor tick. Empty = the cron URL is disabled (fail closed).
# CRON_SECRET=

# ── Where it listens ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOST is SET here, not commented out, and that is deliberate.
#
# Node binds every interface when HOST is unset. The README tells a new
# operator to `cp .env.example .env`, so leaving this commented meant the
# documented first install answered on the local network -- including /desk and
# the Server page, which can restart services on the machine. A gate audit
# booted it exactly that way and reached the desk from another address on the
# LAN.
#
# It gets worse in company. Sign-in throttling buckets by the visitor's address,
# read from `cf-connecting-ip`. Cloudflare sets that header at its edge and a
# visitor coming through the tunnel cannot forge it -- but anything reaching
# the port directly can. Measured on this machine: 25 wrong passwords from one
# address gets 10 refusals then blocked; the same 25 with a rotating header
# gets 24 through. Binding to the loopback address is what keeps that path
# closed, so it is the default rather than an optional hardening step.
#
# Change it only if something else fronts the app AND you trust that front end
# to set the visitor's real address.
#
# This applies to the BUILT server (`npm start`). `npm run dev` defaults to
# 127.0.0.1:8080 (this PC only) -- hard-coded port in vite.config.ts, and it
# ignores PORT. Run `npm run dev:lan` for 0.0.0.0:8080 (phone/LAN testing).
# See docs/setup.md, "What you need", for why.
HOST=127.0.0.1
# PORT=3000
# Applies to the BUILT server only -- see the note above HOST for why `npm run dev` ignores it.

# ── Everything else the code reads ────────────────────────────────────────────
# An audit found this file was not the inventory docs/setup.md claims it is
# (TW-006). These are the remaining variables the application actually consults.
# Grok-broker settings are preview-only and are listed for completeness.
#
# Model routing
# TOWNREPORTER_PLANNER_MODEL=   # override the Dark Desk planner model on any provider
# TOWNREPORTER_EDITORIAL_MODEL= # override Claude Opinion only (default claude-opus-5)
# EDITORIAL_TIMEOUT_MS=2700000  # per research/writing pass, not the whole editorial; see docs/setup.md
# OPENAI_API_KEY=               # only read as a fallback for an OpenAI-compatible gateway
#
# The paper's own address, used for canonical URLs, the sitemap and the feed
# PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://your-paper.example
#
# Extra origins Better Auth should trust (comma separated). Rarely needed.
# BETTER_AUTH_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=
#
# Tests
# RUN_LIVE_MODEL_TESTS=1        # opt in to the live model evaluation; see npm run test:live-model
#
# Admin connection used ONLY by sign-in-throttle.test.ts, leave-desk.test.ts
# and search-index.test.ts to create/drop their own scratch databases. Unset
# locally, these fall back to postgres://postgres@127.0.0.1:5433/postgres and
# skip (with a reason) if nothing answers there. CI sets this to its Postgres
# service container; see .github/workflows/ci.yml and
# src/lib/test-support/pg-admin.ts.
# TEST_POSTGRES_ADMIN_URL=postgres://postgres@127.0.0.1:5433/postgres
#
# Stream a test-spawned server's own stdout/stderr into the test log. A loader
# failure degrades to an empty page, so without this the only symptom of a
# server-side error is "the test found nothing". CI sets it for the Postgres
# integration job.
# PG_TEST_SERVER_LOGS=1
#
# Preview only (grok.me). Ignored on a self-host.
# GROK_PROJECT_ID=
# GROK_AUTH_ISSUER=
# GROK_AUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GROK_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GROK_GATE_ORIGIN=
# GROK_CONNECTORS_URL=
# GROK_CONNECTOR_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# GROK_API_KEY=
# Multiplayer (Dark Desk co-investigating). Comma-separated STUN server URLs
# for ICE gathering. Defaults to Google's public STUN if unset.
# VITE_STUN_URLS=stun:stun.l.google.com:19302,stun:stun1.l.google.com:19302
#
# How a reader reaches the editor. Printed on About and on Corrections, and
# nowhere at all while it is unset -- the Corrections page used to tell readers
# to write the editor from the About page, which carried no address of any
# kind, so the paper promised a way in and ended at a wall.
# Read at BUILD time, so set it before npm run build.
# VITE_TOWNREPORTER_EDITOR_EMAIL=you@example.org

# Headless Chromium (JS-heavy source rendering, e.g. Municode/PrimeGov pages).
# Launches WITH its OS-level renderer sandbox by default, because that sandbox
# is what keeps a hostile page's JS from reaching this process's privileges.
# Some environments (root user, minimal containers without the right
# namespaces/seccomp) can't start the sandbox at all -- Chromium just fails to
# launch there. render-fetch.ts already falls back to --no-sandbox once and
# logs a warning if that happens, so a bad environment degrades loudly instead
# of going dark. Set this only if you know your deploy target is one of those
# environments and want to skip the failed attempt and the warning:
# TOWNREPORTER_CHROMIUM_NO_SANDBOX=1
